The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
orig_pte = pte_mknoncont(ptep_get(ptep));
if (pte_val(orig_pte) == pte_val(entry))
	return 0;
```
Reason:
`ptep_get()` gathers Access Flag and Dirty bits from all sub-PTEs in the CONT block by ORing them together. If a sibling sub-PTE has been updated (e.g., made dirty), the gathered value may show the AF/dirty/write state as already matching `entry`, even though the target sub-PTE (the one being updated) still has `PTE_RDONLY` set or lacks `PTE_AF`. This causes a false no-op (return 0), leaving the target sub-PTE unmodified. For page-table walkers that evaluate each descriptor individually (e.g., CPUs without DBM or SMMUs without HTTU), the target sub-PTE continues to trigger faults, leading to an infinite fault loop.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
static bool contpte_all_subptes_match_access_flags(pte_t *ptep, pte_t entry)
{
	pte_t *cont_ptep = contpte_align_down(ptep);
	const pteval_t cmp_mask = PTE_RDONLY | PTE_AF | PTE_WRITE | PTE_DIRTY;
	pteval_t entry_cmp = pte_val(entry) & cmp_mask;
	int i;
	for (i = 0; i < CONT_PTES; i++) {
		pteval_t pte_cmp = pte_val(__ptep_get(cont_ptep + i)) & cmp_mask;
		if (pte_cmp != entry_cmp)
			return false;
	}
	return true;
}
...
if (contpte_all_subptes_match_access_flags(ptep, entry))
	return 0;
...
orig_pte = pte_mknoncont(__ptep_get(ptep));
```
Reason:
The new helper `contpte_all_subptes_match_access_flags()` checks each sub-PTE individually using `__ptep_get()` (which returns the raw per-PTE value without gathering) against the same bits consumed by `__ptep_set_access_flags()` (AF, dirty, write permission). Only when all sub-PTEs in the CONT block already match the required state is the operation considered a no-op. This prevents false no-ops caused by sibling updates. Additionally, `orig_pte` is now obtained from `__ptep_get(ptep)` (the raw target PTE) instead of `ptep_get()`, ensuring the write-bit unfold decision is based on the actual state of the target sub-PTE, not a gathered view.

